35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is a sobering reminder that people are the most fragile part of any project. Some skills have vanished, while others are plentiful.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I found that computing is still heavily subsidized, but the cost of physical lab space and animal care has become a burden on the awardee.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,Oh no! Some of the big machines are barely working because there's no one to fix them. But the animal facility is actually doing great!,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"How fascinating. It seems the biological ""surprises"" are far more disruptive than the technical ones. The schedule overruns are quite telling.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data confirms my suspicion. The most ambitious ""breakthrough"" projects often stall, while the plainer, utility-driven ones actually deliver.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Kenji, your finding echoes mine. If the machines are broken and the technicians are gone, the most brilliant proposal is but a dream.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"That's a worrying balance. If we fund a project that needs expensive animal work, the grant might run out of money much faster than expected.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"Exactly! We have to be careful. If a project needs the spectroscopy suite, they might just be waiting around for months doing nothing.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. I've noticed that projects relying on live biological systems are far more prone to these ""unforeseen"" delays. We should favor stability.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,Stability is key. I've seen that projects focusing on enduring utility—tools and repositories—have a much higher success rate than those chasing a single mechanism.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,Let us remember that a project is a living thing. It needs a healthy environment to grow. We must look for the path of least resistance.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,I think we should prioritize projects that can leverage the subsidized computing resources. It's the safest way to ensure the money lasts.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I agree! Let's pick something that doesn't need the broken machines. If they can use the animal facility or computers, they'll actually get work done!","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"I'm inclined to agree. A project that avoids the biological ""chaos"" of breeding colonies or the fragility of shared hardware is a project that finishes.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"I will support a candidate that focuses on a deliverable that is historically reliable. We cannot afford another ""confident pick"" that quietly stalls.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
